
======================================================================================================
f_r/f_t FOR A REAL EXPONENTIAL DISC -- the deciding number for the differential-drag escape
======================================================================================================
  a0 = c H_Lambda/Z, Z = 5.78881 -> 9.3600e-11 m/s^2 (canonical); alt 1.1300e-10.
  kappa = 1/2 is Carl's and stays FITTED. NOTHING here depends on a0 or on the footing:
  f_r/f_t = I_x/(R J0) with kappa, G and c^2 cancelling identically, so the result is a pure
  geometric ratio and the footing fork cannot move it.

======================================================================================================
S1. TWO ANALYTIC LIMIT CONTROLS -- the machinery must reproduce cases known in closed form
======================================================================================================
  (a) A UNIFORM SHEET (Sigma = const) has no radial gradient, so <s cos psi> = 0 by the psi
      integral alone and f_r/f_t must be EXACTLY 1. If the code cannot get 1 here it is wrong.
      computed f_r/f_t = 1.000000000000   (quadrature error estimate 7.0e-14)
  [OK  ] uniform sheet returns f_r/f_t = 1.0000000000, i.e. 1 to better than 1e-8 -- the integration scheme and the field-point-centred change of variables are both correct

  (b) MASS CONCENTRATED FAR INSIDE the field point must give f_r/f_t -> 0, because a compact
      central source has x'_x ~ 0 and contributes to J0 but not to I_x.
         R/R_d      f_r/f_t
           1.0     0.439091
           3.0     0.264729
          10.0     0.032622
          30.0     0.003356
         100.0     0.000300
  [OK  ] the ratio falls monotonically toward 0 as the field point moves outside the mass (reaching 0.0003 at R = 100 R_d) -- the limit is right and the estimator is not rigged

======================================================================================================
S3. THE DECISION THRESHOLD, DERIVED
======================================================================================================
  With full translational drag (f_t = 1) the m=1 contamination vanishes and
      lambda = v_rel/v_orb = 1 - f_r/f_t.
  The cost in the deep regime is |d log10 g_obs| = |p * log10(lambda)| dex, with the
  dictionary exponent p = 1/2 (p=1 reading) or 1 (p=2 reading, the corpus's own witness
  action). Inside the 0.2232 dex budget requires:
      p=1: lambda > 0.3578  =>  f_r/f_t < 0.6422
      p=2: lambda > 0.5981  =>  f_r/f_t < 0.4019
  [OK  ] the p=1 threshold (0.6422) is looser than p=2 (0.4019), as it must be since p=2 doubles the sensitivity to lambda -- the thresholds are consistent

======================================================================================================
S4. *** THE NUMBER, FOR REAL DISCS ***
======================================================================================================
  Thin exponential disc Sigma ~ exp(-R/R_d). Milky Way: R_d ~ 2.6 kpc and R_0 = 8.2 kpc give
  R/R_d = 3.15. SPARC discs span roughly R/R_d = 1-5 over their measured rotation curves.

       R/R_d    f_r/f_t    lambda   dex p=1   dex p=2         inside budget?
        1.00    0.43909   0.56091    0.1256    0.2511               p=1 only
        2.00    0.35095   0.64905    0.0939    0.1877                   BOTH
        3.00    0.26473   0.73527    0.0668    0.1336                   BOTH
        3.15    0.25278   0.74722    0.0633    0.1266                   BOTH
        4.00    0.19201   0.80799    0.0463    0.0926                   BOTH
        5.00    0.13684   0.86316    0.0320    0.0639                   BOTH

  MILKY WAY (R/R_d = 3.15):  f_r/f_t = 0.2528,  lambda = 0.7472,
    cost 0.0633 dex (p=1) = 0.28x budget;  0.1266 dex (p=2) = 0.57x budget
  [OK  ] f_r/f_t < 1 at every radius (0.4391 max) -- forced, because an exponential disc has more mass INTERIOR to the field point, so <s cos psi> < 0. Rotation therefore drags the frame LESS efficiently than translation does, for a purely geometric reason
  [OK  ] f_r/f_t falls monotonically with R/R_d (0.4391 -> 0.1368) -- the further out the field point, the more the 1/|x-x'| kernel is dominated by the central mass whose lever arm x'_x is small. A structural trend, not a fitted one

  BUDGET COMPARISON, REPORTED NOT ASSERTED: 6/6 radii inside the p=1
  budget (threshold 0.6422); 5/6 inside p=2 (threshold 0.4019).
  [OK  ] the tally is 6/6 (p=1) and 5/6 (p=2) and is printed above as computed -- this check deliberately asserts NO direction, because my prior expectation (f_r/f_t ~ 0.6-0.8, escape killed) was WRONG and an assertion either way would have encoded it

======================================================================================================
S5. SENSITIVITY: a NON-ROTATING bulge helps the escape -- how much?
======================================================================================================
  All matter translates with the galaxy, so a bulge adds to J0 (hence f_t). But a
  dispersion-supported bulge does NOT rotate, so it does NOT add to I_x. That lowers
  f_r/f_t and therefore HELPS the escape. Test how large a bulge would be needed.
  Modelled as a compact central component of scale 0.2 R_d carrying mass fraction q.

     bulge frac q    f_r/f_t    lambda   dex p=2   inside p=2 budget?
             0.00    0.25278   0.74722    0.1266                 True
             0.10    0.22969   0.77031    0.1133                 True
             0.20    0.20614   0.79386    0.1003                 True
             0.30    0.18214   0.81786    0.0873                 True
             0.50    0.13270   0.86730    0.0618                 True
             0.70    0.08124   0.91876    0.0368                 True
  [OK  ] the ratio falls monotonically with bulge fraction (0.2528 -> 0.0812) -- forced, since a non-rotating bulge adds to J0 but not to I_x. So a bulge HELPS the escape, and that direction is reported rather than buried

  Note the direction: MORE bulge means a SMALLER ratio, hence lambda closer to 1 and a
  SMALLER cost. Bulges make the differential escape EASIER, not harder.

======================================================================================================
VERDICT
======================================================================================================
  *** f_r/f_t = 0.2528 at the Milky Way solar radius (R/R_d = 3.15), and 0.4391 (R/R_d=1) down to 0.1368 (R/R_d=5). ***

  *** THE ANSWER CAME OUT THE OPPOSITE WAY FROM MY EXPECTATION, AND IT FAVOURS THE ESCAPE. ***
  I predicted f_r/f_t ~ 0.6-0.8 and said ~1 would kill the differential escape while ~0.5
  would keep it. The computed value is 0.14-0.44 -- SMALLER than the escape even needs. So:
    lambda = 1 - f_r/f_t = 0.7472 at the Milky Way,
    cost 0.0633 dex (p=1) = 0.28x budget;  0.1266 dex (p=2) = 0.57x budget.
  INSIDE the budget on BOTH dictionaries at the Milky Way, and inside on p=1 at every radius
  tested. Only the innermost point (R/R_d = 1) sits marginally outside on p=2.

  AND IT SURVIVES FOR A CLEAN PHYSICAL REASON, not an accident of parameters. The 1/|x-x'|
  kernel weights the WHOLE galaxy, and it is dominated by the central mass -- which sits at
  displacement -R from the field point and therefore has lever arm x'_x ~ 0. So the central
  mass contributes fully to J0 (translation) and almost nothing to I_x (rotation):
  *** ROTATION DRAGS THE FRAME FAR LESS EFFICIENTLY THAN TRANSLATION DOES, GEOMETRICALLY. ***
  That is exactly the differential behaviour the escape requires, and it is forced rather
  than tuned. A non-rotating bulge makes it EASIER still (S5, monotone).

  WHAT THIS MEANS FOR THE CORNER, stated plainly and against my own prior direction: the
  differential-drag escape is NOT closed by the geometry. The last locally-dragged-frame
  corner therefore stands or falls ENTIRELY on the two other legs, both of which are
  unchanged by this file:
    * the MEASURED magnitude bound -- solar-system frame-dragging, shortfall >= 2.11e5; and
    * the SCREENING SQUEEZE -- potential-keyed screening structurally unavailable, and
      density-keyed screening predicting a stars-vs-gas split at ~14x the recorded agreement
      (that leg soft: the 3% is a remembered order-of-magnitude, not a fit).
  So the corner is still SQUEEZED BUT ALIVE, and one of the three legs I expected to help
  close it has turned out to push the other way.

  WHAT IS SETTLED, and it is the debt I owed after naming this integral twice: f_r/f_t is NOT
  a free parameter. It is a computed geometric ratio, 0.14-0.44 across real disc radii,
  obtained with NO a0, NO footing choice and NO RAR budget -- two analytic limit controls
  (uniform sheet -> exactly 1; mass concentrated inside -> 0) and a monotonicity control pass.

  UNCHANGED: the pincer (Theorem 3 forbids all local L; Theorem 8's argument mismatch stands),
  no action reproducing the closure off circles exists, a0 is not derived, kappa = 1/2 stays
  FITTED, and no door is declared closed.
======================================================================================================
